{"data": [{"author_id": "1873277766", "created_at": "2026-11-03T21:49:12.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2117735874905469034"], "entities": {"urls": [{"display_url": "x.com/BirthGauge/sta…", "end": 300, "expanded_url": "https://twitter.com/BirthGauge/status/2117734304419664076", "start": 277, "url": "https://t.co/wtABEouAxI"}]}, "id": "2117735874905469034", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 4, "impression_count": 608, "like_count": 13, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "referenced_tweets": [{"id": "2117734304419664076", "type": "quoted"}], "text": "That post made me wonder whether any country in history had a larger within-country fertility difference than Czechoslovakia in 1930. In Prague, the TFR was around 1.0, while it was 4.9 in Ruthenia (present-day Zakarpattia Oblast in Ukraine). Czechoslovakia had a TFR of 2.55. https://t.co/wtABEouAxI", "url": "https://x.com/BirthGauge/status/2117735874905469034"}, {"author_id": "1873277766", "created_at": "2026-11-03T21:42:58.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2117734304419664076"], "entities": {"urls": [{"display_url": "pic.x.com/A6vHfytWin", "end": 302, "expanded_url": "https://x.com/BirthGauge/status/2117734304419664076/photo/1", "media_key": "3_2117732880667332608", "start": 279, "url": "https://t.co/A6vHfytWin"}]}, "id": "2117734304419664076", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 5, "impression_count": 1673, "like_count": 46, "quote_count": 1, "reply_count": 1, "retweet_count": 3}, "text": "95 years of demographic change, Prague edition.\nIn 1930, the Czech capital was dominated by people in their 20s, while WWI had caused a big gap in the 10-15 year olds. The TFR was very low, around 1.0 children per woman.\nToday there are a lot more older people, but the majority https://t.co/A6vHfytWin", "url": "https://x.com/BirthGauge/status/2117734304419664076"}, {"author_id": "1810013319794966530", "created_at": "2026-11-03T21:19:47.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2117728469690060958"], "entitie